  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "frame_get_lazychunk",
    "file": "c-blosc2/blosc/frame.c",
    "description": "In the contiguous in-memory frame path, the chunk-boundary validation at line 2028 is incorrectly guarded by `rc`: `blosc2_cbuffer_sizes()` returns 0 when it successfully parses the attacker-controlled chunk header, so an otherwise valid header whose claimed `cbytes` extends past the frame is not rejected. `frame_decompress_chunk()` then passes that untrusted claimed size to decompression although the backing allocation is shorter. Here the crafted extended chunk sets `BLOSC2_USEDICT`; `initialize_context_decompression()` consequently believes the dictionary-size field is within `srcsize` and `sw32_()` reads 4 bytes beyond the actual frame allocation. The fix should handle a negative size-parser result separately and, on success, unconditionally validate the full chunk extent from `chunk_header_offset` against the real frame length using overflow-safe arithmetic.",
    "conditions": "An attacker supplies an in-memory contiguous frame accepted by `blosc2_schunk_from_buffer`, with a valid chunk offset/header but a chunk-header `cbytes` value larger than the bytes remaining in the frame. Because successful parsing makes `rc == 0`, `frame_get_lazychunk()` skips its extent check and returns the chunk pointer plus the oversized claimed length. For this PoV, the chunk is an extended, non-memcpy chunk with the dictionary-used flag set and header/block values that make the claimed source size appear sufficient through the bstarts/dictionary-size position; decompression reaches `initialize_context_decompression()` and reads the dictionary size beyond the actual input buffer."
  },
